{"name":"Acapulco Sun","hex":"#eb8a44","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sun","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Sundanese."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The star that is closest to the Earth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sun"}],"entries":[{"title":"Changes (The Monkees album)","blocks":[{"text":"Changes is the ninth studio album by the American pop rock band the Monkees, released in 1970 by Colgems Records. The album was issued after Michael Nesmith's exit from the band, leaving only Micky Dolenz and Davy Jones to fulfill the recording contract they had signed in the mid-1960s. Changes was their last new album for Colgems Records and the group's last album of all new material until Pool It!, released in 1987."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f6/Monkees-Changes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Changes_(The_Monkees_album)"},{"title":"Acapulco","blocks":[{"text":"Acapulco de Juárez, commonly called Acapulco, is a city and major seaport in the state of Guerrero on the Pacific coast of Mexico, 380 kilometres (240 mi) south of Mexico City. Located on a deep, semicircular bay, Acapulco has been a port since the early colonial period in Mexico's history. It is a port of call for shipping and cruise lines running between Panama and San Francisco, California. The city of Acapulco is the largest in the state, larger than the state capital Chilpancingo. Acapulco is also Mexico's largest beach and balneario resort city. Acapulco de Juárez, Guerrero is the municipal seat of the municipality of Acapulco, Guerrero."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Skyscrapers_in_Acapulco_Diamante.jpg/330px-Skyscrapers_in_Acapulco_Diamante.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acapulco"},{"title":"Sun","blocks":[{"text":"The Sun is the star located at the centre of the Solar System. It is a massive sphere of hot plasma, heated to incandescence by nuclear fusion reactions in its core, radiating the energy from its surface mainly as visible light and infrared radiation, with 10% at ultraviolet energies. It is the main source of energy for life on Earth. The Sun has been an object of veneration in many cultures and a central subject of astronomical research since antiquity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg/330px-The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Acapulco3.jpg/500px-Acapulco3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Acapulco3.jpg/1280px-Acapulco3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Acapulco3.jpg/500px-Acapulco3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcapulco3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg/500px-LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg/1280px-LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg/500px-LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg?ut